d taking metformin twice a day.\n\nShe has been sleeping well and is able to sleep a full 8 hours. She denies thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Grip strength equal bilaterally. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy. No carotid bruits appreciable.\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate. Grade 2/6 systolic ejection fraction murmur. No gallops or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Good pulses.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Ortiz is a 24-year-old female patient with a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. She is here today for her yearly exam.\n\nDiabetes mellitus type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood glucose levels have been uncontrolled. She is still taking metformin twice a day, but does admit to dietary indiscretion with an increase in her sweets intake.. Her most recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to check her fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. She will follow up in about 2 months, at which time we will discuss further treatment recommendations.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Appears to be relatively stable.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing well, thanks.\n\n[doctor]: Great. I\'m glad to hear that. You\'re here for your annual exam, correct?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. So, let\'s start with your medical history. You have a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. How have you been managing those conditions?\n\n[patient]: Yeah, I\'ve been taking my medication as prescribed and my blood pressure has been good. But my diabetes has been a bit tricky lately.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How has it been tricky?\n\n[patient]: Well, I recently started working at a bakery and I\'ve been trying new recipes and eating more sweets. I know I shouldn\'t, but it\'s hard to resist.\n\n[doctor]: I understand. It can be challenging to resist the temptation of sweets, especially when you\'re around them all the time. Have you noticed any changes in your blood sugar levels since you started working at the bakery?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my blood sugars have been fluctuating a lot. I\'ve been trying to cut back on the sweets, but it\'s hard.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you been taking your medication as prescribed, including the metformin?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking it twice a day and haven\'t missed any doses.\n\n[doctor]: Great to hear that. How about your sleep? Have you been sleeping well?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been sleeping well. I can get a full 8 hours of sleep each night.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Do you have any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t have any thoughts like that.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, thank you for letting me know. Now, let\'s do a quick physical exam. Let me check your neck. It looks supple without any swelling or lumps. I\'m not hearing any abnormal sounds in your lungs either. Your heart sounds regular and there\'s a mild murmur, but that\'s not uncommon. Your pulses are good.\n\n[patient]: Okay, everything sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Your grip strength is equal bilaterally and you seem neurologically intact. Now, let\'s move on to your medical results. Your hemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\n[patient]: Oh, that\'s not good to hear.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it indicates that your diabetes is not under control. We need to work on getting that under control. You\'re currently taking metformin twice a day. I recommend you check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. We\'ll discuss further treatment recommendations when you come back in about 2 months.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Your blood pressure seems to be stable, but we\'ll keep an eye on it. You\'re currently taking lisinopril 10 mg once daily. You\'ll continue with that.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about your treatment plan?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Good. It\'s important to follow the plan and monitor your blood glucose levels. We\'ll see you in about 2 months to reassess. Do you have any questions or concerns before we wrap up?\n\n[patient]: No, I\'m good. Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and see you in 2 months.\n\n[patient]: Thanks, bye.\n\n[doctor]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello, Beverly. Can you hear me OK? Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me, please? \nPatient: Yes, I can hear you fine. My name is Beverly, and my date of birth is August 25th, 1998.\nDoctor: OK, great. So, Beverly, how are you doing today? Any problems?\nPatient: I\'m doing fine, thank you.\nDoctor: OK. And can you tell me a little bit about why you\'re here today?\nPatient: I\'m just here for my annual check-up.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. So, have you had any problems recently?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you working at the moment?\nPatient: Yes, I\'m working at a bakery.\nDoctor: OK. And how\'s that going?\nPatient: It\'s going fine.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any problems with your diabetes recently?\nPatient: Well, my blood sugars have been up and down, but I\'ve been trying to eat better and stuff.\nDoctor: OK. And how\'s your blood pressure been?\nPatient: My blood pressure has been fine.\nDoctor: OK. And are you taking your medications regularly?\nPatient: Yes, I am.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any problems with your diabetes, like any symptoms, numbness in your feet, any vision problems?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems sleeping?\nPatient: I sleep fine.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your mood?\nPatient: No, I\'m fine.\nDoctor: OK. OK. And can you tell me a little bit more about your diet?\nPatient: I\'ve been trying to eat better. I\'ve been trying to avoid sweets.\nDoctor: OK. And what kind of sweets have you been eating?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve been eating a lot of pastries and cakes at work.\nDoctor: OK. And how often are you eating those?\nPatient: I eat them every day.\nDoctor: OK. And how many a day?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. A few a day.\nDoctor: OK. And how much sugar are you having in your diet?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. I don\'t really count.\nDoctor: OK. And how much sugar are you having in your diet?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any problems with your blood sugar levels?\nPatient: My blood sugar levels have been up and down.\nDoctor: OK. And how high are your blood sugar levels?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. I don\'t really check them.\nDoctor: OK. And how often are you checking your blood sugar levels?\nPatient: I check them once a week.\nDoctor: OK. And how high are your blood sugar levels?\nPatient: Um, they\'re usually around 200.\nDoctor: OK. And have you been feeling any symptoms from your diabetes?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been feeling any symptoms.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your vision?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been having any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your feet?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been having any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your kidneys?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been having any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your skin?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been having any problems.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your