iate professional vocabulary, though might benefit from expanded patient-friendly explanations.\n   - Response B employs medical terminology accurately but overuses technical jargon. Additionally, the simplicity and brevity expected from patient responses were missing, often echoing medical terms awkwardly.\n   - [RESULT] A\n\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n   - Response A aligns its guidance with the notes, recommending Bentyl and Elavil as outlined. It emphasizes dietary suggestions and advises on potential triggers, although it's slightly inconsistent with comprehensive lifestyle suggestions.\n   - Response B often departs from the original notes, delving into speculative advice such as dietary changes and supplemental probiotics without strong correlation to documented recommendations.\n   - [RESULT] A\n\nAlthough both responses attempt to reflect key elements from the medical notes, Response A demonstrates a stronger adherence to the prescribed score rubric. It consistently adheres more closely to the SOAP notes, accurately translates professional terminology, maintains a natural flow, and encompasses crucial elements, making it the preferable response.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}}], "created": 1734276934, "model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_9faba9f038", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 555, "prompt_tokens": 6590, "total_tokens": 7145, "completion_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0}}}


