.\n\n[patient] That\'s good.\n\n[doctor] Your cranial nerves are intact. Pupils are equal, extraocular movements are normal, face is symmetric, and there\'s no dysarthria.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Your motor function is normal for bulk and strength, but your coordination is slow for finger-to-nose.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] Let\'s review your CT scan from 10/15/2020. It shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. There\'s a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. The radiologist didn\'t notice this, but it\'s not causing any symptoms.\n\n[patient] That\'s interesting.\n\n[doctor] Based on all this, I\'m going to continue to monitor your condition. You\'ve made some improvement with your hydrocephalus, but there\'s still a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. I don\'t believe this tiny amount of fluid is causing symptoms, and the radiologist didn\'t notice it.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Unfortunately, I think it\'s unlikely you\'ll be able to drive again. However, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they can pass a driver\'s safety evaluation. If you\'re interested in trying, I can recommend such a test. You\'re also welcome to enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation, but I think it\'s unlikely you\'ll pass the test.\n\n[patient] I understand.\n\n[doctor] I\'m going to leave the shunt setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing it.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] You\'ll need to follow up in three months. Any other questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor] Great. I\'ll see you in three months. Take care and thank you for coming in.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello?\nPatient: Hello?\nDoctor: Hello. Good morning. Can you hear me