[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay, thanks. I\'ve been having some abdominal pain.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How long have you been experiencing this pain?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been a few weeks now. It\'s on my right side, and it\'s been pretty constant.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Have you noticed any other symptoms like nausea or vomiting?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve had some nausea, but no vomiting.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s helpful to know. Do you have any history of diabetes?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I have type 2 diabetes.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How do you manage it?\n\n[patient]: I manage it through diet and exercise.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. Do you have any hobbies or things you enjoy doing?\n\n[patient]: I like nature photography.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds lovely. Do you have any family history of any medical conditions?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my parents both had cholecystectomies, so they have a history of cholelithiasis.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Let\'s go through a quick review of your systems. How have you been feeling overall? Any fever?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve had some low-grade fevers, but nothing too severe.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, and any gastrointestinal issues?\n\n[patient]: The abdominal pain and nausea, like I mentioned.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, and how\'s your breathing?\n\n[patient]: My respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s within normal limits. Your heart rate is?\n\n[patient]: 68 beats per minute.\n\n[doctor]: And your oxygen saturation?\n\n[patient]: It\'s 99% on room air.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. Any fever or chills?\n\n[patient]: No, I\'ve been afebrile.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. Can you tell me if you have any pain when I press on your abdomen?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I have some pain on the right side.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, I\'ll check that further. Let me listen to your lungs and heart. Your lungs sound clear and equal bilaterally. Your heart sounds regular and there are no murmurs, clicks, or rubs. Now, let me examine your abdomen. It feels soft and not distended. There\'s some slight guarding on the right upper quadrant, but no rebound tenderness. I can also feel some Murphy\'s sign. There\'s no peritoneal irritation.\n\n[patient]: What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means your gallbladder is inflamed, and there\'s some thickening. We\'ll need to do an ultrasound to confirm.\n\n[patient]: Okay, what\'s the ultrasound show?\n\n[doctor]: The ultrasound shows multiple gallstones in your gallbladder, and there\'s some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see. What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means you have mild cholecystitis with gallstones. We\'ll need to consider surgery to remove your gallbladder.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'m not sure about that. What are the risks?\n\n[doctor]: The surgery is a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, which is a minimally invasive procedure. It usually takes about 1 to 1.5 hours and you\'ll likely spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We\'ll also advise you to avoid high-fat foods after the surgery and eat foods high in fiber.\n\n[patient]: That sounds serious. What about my diabetes?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll continue to monitor your diabetes as you\'ve been doing, but we\'ll recommend you refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period. However, you can return to walking on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I understand. What\'s the next step?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll schedule the surgery for 1 week from today. You\'ll need to follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand. Thank you for explaining everything.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. We\'ll take good care of you. See you in a week.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I\'ll see you then.\n\n[doctor]: Take care, bye.\n\n[patient]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi, hello? Hello?\nPatient: Hello.\nDoctor: Hello. Uh, my name is Doctor so and so and I\'m a general surgeon. Um, how are you doing today?\nPatient: I\'m good, I\'m good.\nDoctor: Good. Can you confirm your date of birth for me, please?\nPatient: Um, 19th of March, 1975.\nDoctor: And your address, please?\nPatient: Um, 12, 12, 12, 12.\nDoctor: And your contact details, please?\nPatient: Um, my mobile number is 7, 7, 7, 7, 7.\nDoctor: And your email address, please?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t have an email address.\nDoctor: Okay, that\'s fine. Uh, what\'s your name, please?\nPatient: Um, my name is, uh, John.\nDoctor: Okay. Uh, John, um, um, how can I help you today?\nPatient: Uh, I\'ve had, I\'ve had some abdominal pain.\nDoctor: Okay. Okay. Um, can you describe the pain for me, please?\nPatient: Uh, it\'s just been, it\'s been a couple of weeks now, and it\'s been getting progressively worse. Um, it\'s on the right side, and it\'s been quite painful. Uh, I\'ve been having a bit of nausea, uh, and I\'ve been feeling a bit feverish.\nDoctor: Okay. Um, can you describe the pain to me, please?\nPatient: Uh, it\'s a dull ache, but it\'s been getting worse. It\'s been getting worse over the last couple of weeks.\nDoctor: Um, have you had any vomiting?\nPatient: No, no vomiting.\nDoctor: Okay. Um, have you had any diarrhea?\nPatient: No, no diarrhea.\nDoctor: Okay. Um, have you had any change in bowel movements?\nPatient: No, no change in bowel movements.\nDoctor: Okay