, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing okay, thanks. I\'ve been having some back pain.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Can you tell me more about the back pain you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient]: Sure. It started about four months ago when I was moving boxes out of my son\'s college dorm. I felt a tingling in my right toes and it lasted for about an hour.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Did the tingling resolve after that hour?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it did. But after that, I felt like I couldn\'t support my upper body weight. I also felt weak in my legs when carrying heavy objects.\n\n[doctor]: I understand. How about the pain in your lower back? Can you describe it to me?\n\n[patient]: It\'s a sharp pain, especially when I turn a certain way or roll over in my sleep. It goes away if I reposition myself.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you seen anyone else for this pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I went to urgent care. They referred me to get an MRI, but I haven\'t been able to get it done because of insurance issues.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How have you been managing the pain so far?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been using Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice. The ibuprofen seems a bit more helpful than the Tylenol, but it doesn\'t really relieve my symptoms.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s helpful to know. Have you experienced any upset stomach with the ibuprofen?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any issues with stomach upset.\n\n[doctor]: Great. You mentioned you tried physical therapy at your gym, but it didn\'t help. Can you tell me more about that?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I did physical therapy at my gym, but it didn\'t provide any relief from the pain.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. How about any other symptoms like fever, chills, or bladder or bowel issues?\n\n[patient]: No, none of those.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s review your medical history. You mentioned you had a right total knee arthroplasty two years ago. How\'s your knee been doing since the surgery?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been 95% better. I\'m really happy with the results.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great to hear. Now, let\'s move on to your vital signs. Your temperature is normal, your blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, your heart rate is 68 BPM, and your oxygenation is 95% on room air. Everything looks good there.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s do a physical exam. When I examine your neck, I notice a bony protuberance at the C5-C6 area, but it doesn\'t cause any pain when I palpate it. Your lumbar spine is also normal to the touch. However, I do notice some decreased flexion and extension of your lower back. You also have a positive straight leg raise and some spasm in the paraspinal muscles of your lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that\'s what I\'ve been feeling.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. We\'ve also reviewed your x-rays of the lumbar spine, and they didn\'t show any acute bony abnormalities. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, I suspect you might have an acute disc herniation. I\'d like to order an MRI to confirm this.\n\n[patient]: Alright, that sounds like the next step.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, an MRI will give us a better idea of what\'s going on. In the meantime, I\'ve prescribed you meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain. You\'ll also continue with physical therapy, which I\'ve referred you to today. Does that sound okay?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that sounds good. Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, we\'ll get the MRI scheduled and follow up with you once the results are in. Take care and feel better soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I appreciate it.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello, Jeremy. Hi, can you hear me okay?\nPatient: Yeah, yeah.\nDoctor: Great. Hi, can you confirm your name and date of birth for me?\nPatient: Yeah. My name is Jeremy Adams and I was born in 1965.\nDoctor: Okay, great. And can you confirm your address for me?\nPatient: Yeah, I live at 123 Main Street.\nDoctor: Okay, great. And can you confirm your date of birth for me again?\nPatient: 1965.\nDoctor: Okay, great. Can you tell me a bit about what brings you to the doctor today?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve been having some lower back pain, and it\'s been going on for about four months now. And I\'ve been trying to get it checked out, but I haven\'t been able to get an MRI.\nDoctor: Okay. So, four months of lower back pain. So, can you tell me a bit more about the pain? Is it constant? Is it intermittent? How would you describe it?\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s been pretty constant. It\'s been pretty constant. Um, it\'s more like a dull ache, but it\'s pretty constant. I can\'t really pinpoint when it started, but I think it started after I was moving some boxes out of my son\'s college dorm. And I think I just kind of strained my back or something. I don\'t know. But, um, it\'s been going on for four months now.\nDoctor: OK, and can you describe the pain? Is it in your lower back, upper back, is it in your legs?\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s in my lower back. It\'s more on the right side of my back. It\'s like a dull ache, like I said. It\'s not sharp pain, but it\'s definitely there. And sometimes it\'ll radiate down into my legs.\nDoctor: OK, and when you say it radiates down into your legs, what does that mean?\nPatient: Well, it\'s just like, I\'ll be walking, and I\'ll feel like my legs are giving out, like I\'m going to fall over. And I\'ll feel like I\'m not really able to support my upper body weight, you know? Like I\'ll be lifting something, and I\'ll feel like I\'m going to fall over.\nDoctor: OK, OK. So, you\'re having some weakness in your legs as well, is that correct?\nPatient: Yeah, yeah. Yeah.\nDoctor: OK, and do you have any numbness or tingling in your legs?\nPatient: Yeah, I do. I have numbness and tingling in my toes, yeah.\nDoctor: OK, and how long does that last?\nPatient: Um, it\'s usually about an hour, and then it goes away. So, it\'s like, if I\'m moving something heavy, and I\'ll get this numbness and tingling in my toes, and it\'ll go away after like an hour.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your back before?\nPatient: Um, no. No, not really. I\'ve had some minor back problems, but nothing that\'s been really severe.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And do you have any problems with your bladder or bowel movements?\nPatient: No, no, not really.\nDoctor: OK, and any fevers or chills?\nPatient: No, no, not really.\nDoctor: OK, and any rashes or anything like that?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. So, can you tell me a bit more about your medical history? Do you have any other medical conditions?\nPatient: Um, I had a total knee replacement about two years ago.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And how\'s your knee doing now?\nPatient: It\'s doing great. It\'s doing great. I\'d say it\'s about 95% better than it was before.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And do you have any other medical conditions?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. And any allergies to any medications?\nPatient: Um, no.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any other medical conditions?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. And do you take any medications?\nPatient: Um, I take some ibuprofen for my back pain. And I\'ve been taking some Tylenol, but I don\'t really think it\'s helping that much.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any problems with your stomach with taking ibuprofen?\nPatient: No, not really.\nDoctor: OK. And how long have you been taking ibuprofen for?\nPatient: Um, I think about a month now.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your stomach with taking Tylenol?\nPatient: Um, no, not really.\nDoctor: OK. And how long have you been taking Tylenol for?\nPatient: Um, about a month now.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your stomach with taking either of those medications?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any other medical problems?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any other medical problems?\nPatient: No, no.\nDoctor: OK. And can you tell me a bit more about your physical examination today? So, did you notice anything unusual about your back?\nPatient: Um, no, not really. It just feels like it\'s kind of stiff and sore.\nDoctor: OK. And can you describe your physical examination today? So, what did the doctor do?\nPatient: Um, I think they checked my back, and they did some kind of test, and they said that my back is fine.\nDoctor: OK. And what was the test that they did?\nPatient: Um, I\'m not really sure. I think they just kind of bent me and twisted me and stuff like that.\nDoctor: OK. And did you notice any numbness or tingling in your legs during that examination?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t think so. I don\'t think so.\nDoctor: OK. And what did the doctor say?\nPatient: Um, I think they said that I have a slight herniated disc, and that I need to get an MRI.\nDoctor: OK. And did they mention any treatment for you?\nPatient: Um, yeah. They said I could take some pain medication, and I could go to physical therapy.\nDoctor: OK. And what kind of pain medication did they recommend?\nPatient: Um, I think they said I could take some ibuprofen.\nDoctor: OK. And how much did they recommend?\nPatient: Um, I think they said 15 milligrams.\nDoctor: OK. And did they mention any other treatment for you?\nPatient: Um, yeah. They said I could go to physical therapy.\nDoctor: OK. And how many times a week did they recommend you go?\nPatient: Um, I think they said once a week.\nDoctor: OK. And how long did they recommend you go for?\nPatient: Um, I think they said for four weeks.\nDoctor: OK. And did they mention any other treatment for you?\nPatient: Um, yeah. They said I could try some heat and ice.\nDoctor: OK. And how often did they recommend you use heat and ice?\nPatient: Um, I think they said to use it as needed.\nDoctor: OK. And how often did they recommend you use it as needed?\nPatient: Um, I think they 