on the right. No wheezing right now. There is some diminished breath sounds in the right as well.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema on bilateral lower extremities. No calf tenderness. Negative Homan\'s sign.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: No bruising.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been coughing up blood.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of further testing as his symptoms could be as simple as a pneumonia or something more serious.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work to be done in the ER. I have instructed the patient to go into the ER immediately after this appointment and I will call ahead.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has not been well controlled.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered an updated A1c today. He will increase his metformin to 750 mg twice daily.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure has been running low for approximately 3 weeks. I believe this could be caused by his shortness of breath. I would like to get a better understanding of the cause of his shortness of breath prior to pursuing further treatment for his hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: At this time, he will discontinue the use of Norvasc.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will have him follow up in about 3 weeks to just discuss his diabetes and further treatment recommendations.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay, but I\'ve been having some issues.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, I see. What seems to be the problem?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been having shortness of breath and I\'ve been coughing up some blood.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that doesn\'t sound good. Have you had any fever?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had a fever.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, and have you noticed any bleeding from your gums or any other areas?\n\n[patient]: No, no bleeding.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How about your respiratory symptoms? Any wheezing or cough?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been having shortness of breath and coughing up blood.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Have you had any pain in your knees or lower extremities?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve had a long history of knee pain.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you had any bruising anywhere on your body?\n\n[patient]: No, no bruising.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Let\'s take a look at your vital signs. Your pulse oxygenation is 94 percent and your blood pressure is 120/70 mm Hg. How about you\'re not feeling any chest pain or discomfort?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t feel any chest pain.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let\'s do a physical exam. Your neck is supple, no jugular venous distension, no carotid bruits. Now, let\'s listen to your lungs. I hear some rales and rhonchi, mostly on the right side, but no wheezing. There\'s also some diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Next, I\'ll listen to your heart. It has a regular rate and there\'s a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before. Now, let\'s check your abdomen. It\'s soft. Next, I\'ll check your lower extremities. I see some 1+ nonpitting edema on both legs, but no calf tenderness and no Homan\'s sign.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Any other medical conditions you\'re currently managing?\n\n[patient]: I have diabetes and hypertension.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you been taking any medications?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I take metformin and Norvasc.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, we need to do some further testing to determine the cause of your shortness of breath. I\'ve ordered a chest x-ray, a CT scan, and blood work. You\'ll need to go to the ER immediately after this appointment, and I\'ll call ahead to let them know you\'re coming.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. In addition, your blood sugar hasn\'t been well controlled. I\'ve ordered an updated A1c today. I\'ll increase your metformin to 750 mg twice daily.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll follow that.\n\n[doctor]: Also, your blood pressure has been running low for about three weeks. I think this could be related to your shortness of breath. We need to get a better understanding of the cause of your shortness of breath before pursuing further treatment for your hypertension.\n\n[patient]: I understand.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll have you discontinue the use of Norvasc for now.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll stop taking it.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll have you follow up in about three weeks to discuss your diabetes and further treatment recommendations.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll be back.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Take care and see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello?\nPatient: Hi, thank you for calling.\nDoctor: Hi, good morning. My name is Doctor Smith. Can you please confirm your name for me?\nPatient: Hi, my name is John.\nDoctor: Hi John. I\'m Doctor Smith. Can you please confirm your date of birth for me?\nPatient: Yeah, my date of birth is 23rd of September 1975.\nDoctor: Okay, thank you. And can you please confirm your address for me?\nPatient: Yeah, my address is 123 Main Street.\nDoctor: Okay, thank you. And can you please confirm your contact number for me?\nPatient: Yeah, my contact number is 555-5555.\nDoctor: Okay, thank you. Hi John, what can I help you with today?\nPatient: Um, I\'m having a bit of trouble breathing, and I\'ve been coughing up some blood.\nDoctor: OK, sorry to hear that. Can you please tell me a bit more about your symptoms?\nPatient: Yeah, so I\'ve been having some shortness of breath, and I\'ve been coughing up some blood. Um, I\'m not sure if it\'s a lot or a little bit, but it\'s definitely been happening. And I\'ve been feeling a bit wheezy, and I\'ve been feeling a bit fatigued.\nDoctor: OK, sorry to hear that. Can you please tell me a bit more about your medical history? Do you have any medical conditions?\nPatient: Um, I have diabetes, and I\'ve had that for about 10 years now.\nDoctor: OK, and do you take any medications for that?\nPatient: Yeah, I take metformin. And I\'ve also got high blood pressure, and I take Norvasc.\nDoctor: OK, OK, great. And do you take any other medications?\nPatient: Yeah, I take some inhalers for my asthma, and I also take some painkillers for my knees.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And do you have any allergies to any medications?\nPatient: Yeah, I\'m allergic to sulfa, and I\'m allergic to penicillin.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And do you have any allergies to any foods or anything else?\nPatient: Um, I\'m allergic to shellfish, I think.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And do you have any medical tests that you\'ve had recently?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve had a blood test recently, but I\'m not sure what the results were.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any chest x-rays recently?\nPatient: Um, I think so, but I\'m not sure.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any respiratory problems in the past?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve had asthma, and I\'ve had some wheezing, and I\'ve had some shortness of breath, but it\'s been quite a while ago.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your knees in the past?\nPatient: Um, yeah, I\'ve had some problems with my knees, and I\'ve had some pain in my knees, but it\'s been quite a while ago.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your back in the past?\nPatient: Um, yeah, I\'ve had some back problems, and I\'ve had some pain in my back, but it\'s been quite a while ago.\nDoctor: OK, OK. And have you had any problems with your neck in the past?\nPatient: Um, yeah, I\'ve had some problems with my n