fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the x-ray, it seems like you have medial epicondylitis, which is an inflammation of the tendons on the medial aspect of your elbow. This is likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm. We\'ll treat you conservatively. I recommend you wear a sling on your right arm while you\'re awake to provide increased support. You\'ll also ice your right elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. Take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for the next week. I also advise you to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. How about your diabetes and hypertension? You\'re currently taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice a day. How have you been managing your blood sugars?\n\n[patient]: I check them regularly, and they\'ve been in the 150 to 170 mg/dL range. I sometimes forget to take my metformin, but I try to remember.\n\n[doctor]: I see. I\'m going to increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. It\'s important to take your medication regularly, twice a day. We\'ll also do a hemoglobin A1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. For your hypertension, you\'re currently taking lisinopril 10 mg. You should continue taking this. Your blood pressure is well-controlled. I encourage you to continue with your walking routine.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll keep doing that.\n\n[doctor]: All right. Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient]: No, I think you\'ve covered everything.\n\n[doctor]: Great. You\'ll follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress. Do you have any other medical concerns or questions?\n\n[patient]: No, that\'s it.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Take care, and I\'ll see you in 2 weeks.\n\n[patient]: Thank 