-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you today?\n\n[patient] Good morning, I\'m doing alright, thanks. I\'ve been having some issues with my right knee.\n\n[doctor] I see. What seems to be going on with your knee?\n\n[patient] Well, about two and a half weeks ago, I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle. It\'s been hurting ever since.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Where exactly do you feel the pain?\n\n[patient] It\'s on the outside of my right knee.\n\n[doctor] Does it hurt when you walk?\n\n[patient] No, it doesn\'t. Unless I stand on one foot for a long time. Then it starts to hurt.\n\n[doctor] Okay, and do you feel any pain when you stand with both feet flat on the ground?\n\n[patient] No, it\'s only when I\'m on one foot.\n\n[doctor] How about when you\'re resting and elevating your leg? Does it hurt then?\n\n[patient] No, it doesn\'t.\n\n[doctor] And what about when you\'re sitting or lying down? Does it hurt then?\n\n[patient] No, it\'s only when I\'m standing or standing on one foot.\n\n[doctor] Alright, that\'s helpful. Do you notice any clicking or locking of your knee when you move it?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do feel some clicking when I walk.\n\n[doctor] How about when you rest your foot on your heel? Does it hurt then?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor] Have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your right foot?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have numbness and tingling in my toes, but that\'s not new. It\'s been there for a while.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I see. Have you been doing anything to manage the pain?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been taking ibuprofen and icing my knee four times a day. It helps a bit.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. How much relief do you get from the ibuprofen and icing?\n\n[patient] It gives me relief at night and in the morning, but it doesn\'t last all day.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Have you noticed any impact on your daily activities due to the pain?\n\n[patient] No, I\'m still pretty active during the day, but it doesn\'t affect me too much.\n\n[doctor] Okay, have you been able to do any full squats without pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can do a full squat without pain.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to hear. Have you had any other medical issues or surgeries?\n\n[patient] I\'ve fractured my second toe three times in the last 10 years.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Do you have any other medical conditions or allergies I should know about?\n\n[patient] No, none that I\'m aware of.\n\n[doctor] Great. What do you do for a living?\n\n[patient] I work in sales.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Do you have any hobbies or exercise routines?\n\n[patient] I like to ride my bike for exercise.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s do a quick physical examination. Can you stand up for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Can you lift your foot off the ground and let me check your ankle?\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Everything looks good there. Now, can you let me check your knee?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Can you straighten your knee for me?\n\n[patient] Yes.\n\n[doctor] And now, can you bend it?\n\n[patient] Yes.\n\n[doctor] I see some slight swelling and a slight fluid accumulation in your knee. Does it hurt when I press on the outside of your knee?\n\n[patient] No, it doesn\'t hurt.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. Can you stand with your feet shoulder-width apart and I\'ll check the stability of your knee?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I\'m going to push your knee in. Does it hurt?\n\n[patient] No, it doesn\'t.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s take a look at your X-rays. Everything looks normal, no fractures or dislocations. I\'m going to order an MRI if the pain doesn\'t improve with physical therapy.\n\n[patient] Okay, what\'s the plan?\n\n[doctor] I\'m going to prescribe meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain. I also recommend you start formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. This will help with your pain and improve your knee function. If the pain doesn\'t improve with physical therapy, we\'ll consider the MRI.\n\n[patient] Alright, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor] Great. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, that sounds good. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. I\'ll get the prescription ready for you. Do you have any insurance or identification with you?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have m