-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. The patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. At lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. The patient states that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Not a problem at all. I understand you\'re here for an evaluation of right heel pain. Can you tell me a bit more about how this started?\n\n[patient]: Yes, sure. The pain started about 10 days ago. I\'ve been training for a triathlon and I think it\'s related to that.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, a triathlon. That\'s quite a challenging activity. Where exactly do you feel the pain in your heel?\n\n[patient]: It\'s on the back of my heel, and it radiates up into my calf.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Does the pain get worse when you do certain activities or at any other specific times?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it gets worse when I\'m working out and when I first get up in the morning. If I sit for a long time, it returns when I stand up.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds quite uncomfortable. How often do you engage in these activities?\n\n[patient]: I run 10 kilometers in the morning, and then at lunchtime, I try to bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, I go to the aquatic center and swim for 30 minutes to an hour. I run 5 days a week, and on weekends, I alternate between a long run and a long bike ride. I take one day off from my exercises.\n\n[doctor]: Wow, you\'re quite the athlete. Do you stretch at all?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t stretch at all.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s go through some other systems. Do you have any pain in your left leg?\n\n[patient]: No, no pain there.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Now, I\'m going to do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your musculoskeletal system. Can you tell me if you have any pain when I do this?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do. It hurts when you press on the back of my heel.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, let\'s examine the heel. There\'s some pain with resisted extension, and mild pain with flexion. I\'m also going to do the calcaneal squeeze test. Can you bear with me?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, I\'m going to squeeze your heel now. It\'s a bit tender. There\'s also severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. The Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: What does all that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means that your Achilles tendon is inflamed. This is likely due to overuse from your triathlon training. I\'ve explained to you that it\'s best to take at least a week off from your training to recover. I\'ll also provide a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen your right lower extremities.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good. What else?\n\n[doctor]: I\'m also prescribing you 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for 