-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nNon-healing right foot ulcer.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristina Cooper is a pleasant 77-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a non-healing right foot ulcer. The onset of her wound began 1 year ago with no specific injury. The patient states that it seemed like a blister and she picked at it where it then turned red. The patient was seen by her primary care physician and was prescribed Keflex. She states that the antibiotics improved her symptoms, however they did not resolve them. The patient adds that she was last on Keflex 5 months ago. She denies any pain, however she reports decreased sensation in her foot. The patient also reports increased drainage and fatigue. She states that she has been wearing her regular shoes.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. She states that her blood sugars are running better than they were; however, they are still in the 200\'s. Her last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nSkin: Reports right foot ulcer and drainage.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo fever noted. Vitals look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right foot: There is a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I do see some yellow slough that is present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. I do not see necrosis. No odor. I do not appreciate any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot today reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight diabetic foot ulcer.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that we obtain an ankle brachial index to determine her blood supply to see if we can heal the wound. We discussed doing a debridement in office today, which she agreed to. I have also recommended that the patient be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I have also prescribed the patient collagenase ointment to apply to her wound 1 time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing. I advised the patient that this should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to check on her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m okay, thanks. I\'ve been having some issues with my foot.\n\n[doctor] Oh, I see. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on with your foot?\n\n[patient] Sure. I\'ve had this non-healing ulcer on my right foot for about a year now.\n\n[doctor] I see. When did this start?\n\n[patient] It started about a year ago. I think it was just a blister at first, but then it turned red and I picked at it.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. Did you see a doctor about it initially?\n\n[patient] Yes, I saw my primary care physician. They prescribed me Keflex.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. Did the Keflex help with your symptoms?\n\n[patient] It did improve my symptoms, but it didn\'t completely resolve them.\n\n[doctor] I see. When was the last time you took Keflex?\n\n[patient] It was about 5 months ago.\n\n[doctor] Alright. How has your pain been?\n\n[patient] Actually, I don\'t have any pain.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. But you mentioned some other symptoms. Can you tell me more about the decreased sensation and increased drainage you\'re experiencing?\n\n[patient] Yes, my foot doesn\'t feel like it used to. It\'s like there\'s no feeling there. And the drainage is pretty bad.\n\n[doctor] I see. Have you been wearing your regular shoes?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you noticed any changes in your blood sugar levels?\n\n[patient] My blood sugars have been running a bit better than they were, but they\'re still in the 200\'s.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to hear. Have you had any recent lab work done, such as a hemoglobin A1C?\n\n[patient] Yes, my last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\n[doctor] Thank you for sharing that with me. Let\'s go through a few more things. How have you been feeling overall? Any fatigue?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been feeling quite fatigued.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Now, let\'s do a quick review of your systems. Any fever?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t have a fever.\n\n[doctor] Good. How about your skin? Any issues there?\n\n[patient] I have this right foot ulcer and it\'s draining.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I\'ll take a look. Let\'s get your vitals. Your temperature is normal, and your blood pressure and heart rate are within normal limits. Let\'s move on to the physical exam. Can you show me your foot?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] I see a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There\'s some yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. I don\'t see any fluid collection. There\'s no evidence of necrosis. No odor, and no bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] I\'ve also ordered an X-ray of your right foot. The results show no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] Based on the findings, I\'m going to recommend an ankle brachial index to determine your blood supply and see if we can heal the wound. We can also do a debridement in the office today, which you\'ve agreed to.\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s fine.\n\n[doctor] I\'ve also prescribed collagenase ointment for you to apply to your wound once a day and cover it with a sterile dressing. You\'ll need to continue this until we see the ulcer decrease in size.\n\n[patient] Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] Additionally, I recommend you be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. This will help with the healing process.\n\n[patient] Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll need to follow up in 2 weeks to check on your progress. How does that sound?\n\n[patient] That sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Great. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor] Alright. I\'ll see you in 2 weeks. Take care and don\'t hesitate to reach out if you have any issues.\n\n[patient] Thank you, I will.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care.\n\n[patient] Bye.\n\n[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello there. Can you hear me all right?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: OK. Hi. Hello.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hi. I\'m Doctor.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi. Hi.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello.\nDoctor: Hi.