-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Gutierrez is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand pain.\n\nApproximately 6 months ago, the patient began experiencing intermittent episodes of numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on her right hand. The numbness and tingling has become constant over the past month or so and is accompanied by pain with typing and using a mouse, which she does all day while working on a keyboard. She denies any sleep disturbance associated with her symptoms. Rest and ibuprofen have been the most helpful at providing symptomatic relief.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she works on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great, thanks for coming in. I understand you\'ve been experiencing some issues with your right hand. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I\'ve been having this pain in my right hand. It\'s been happening for about 6 months now.\n\n[doctor]: I see. And when did the pain start?\n\n[patient]: It started about 6 months ago. At first, it was just occasional, but over the past month or so, it\'s been constant.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, okay. How would you describe the pain?\n\n[patient]: It\'s a dull ache, like a burning sensation.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that doesn\'t sound very pleasant. Have you noticed any other symptoms along with the pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve had numbness and tingling in my fingers. It\'s mostly in my thumb, index, and middle fingers.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How often do you experience these symptoms?\n\n[patient]: It\'s constant now, but it was intermittent at first.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s helpful to know. Do you notice any specific activities that trigger the pain or numbness?\n\n[patient]: Yes, when I type or use a mouse, it really hurts.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, I see. Do you have any trouble sleeping because of these symptoms?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any sleep disturbance.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s good to know. Have you tried anything to help with the pain?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been taking ibuprofen, and it helps a bit.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How often do you take the ibuprofen?\n\n[patient]: I take it as needed, usually 3 times a day.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, I see. Let me ask you a few more questions. Do you work on a keyboard all day?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s helpful to know. Do you have any other medical conditions or take any other medications?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t have any other conditions, and I only take ibuprofen.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Let\'s go through a quick review of your systems. Have you noticed any changes in your appetite, weight, or overall energy levels?\n\n[patient]: No, everything seems normal.\n\n[doctor]: How about your bowel habits? Any changes there?\n\n[patient]: No, nothing different.\n\n[doctor]: Have you noticed any muscle or joint pain anywhere else?\n\n[patient]: No, it\'s only in my right hand.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. How about any numbness or tingling anywhere else?\n\n[patient]: No, just in my right hand.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, thank you for that. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. Can you show me your right hand?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m going to gently press on the tendons in your wrist and see if it causes any pain. (examines the patient\'s hand) Hmm, I can see that you have some pain when I flex and extend your wrist. Can you also show me how you grip with your right hand?\n\n[patient]: (demonstrates grip) Like this.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, your grip strength is a bit weaker on the right compared to the left. I\'m also going to do a Tinel\'s sign. (performs the Tinel\'s sign) Yes, this is positive. It suggests compression of the median nerve.\n\n[patient]: What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It suggests that you may have carpal tunnel syndrome. This is a condition where the median nerve, which runs from the forearm into the hand through the carpal tunnel in the wrist, is compressed, causing the symptoms you\'ve described.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see.\n\n[doctor]: We can treat this conservatively with a few different approaches. I recommend you wear a wrist splint at night to help reduce the compression. I\'d also like to increase your ibuprofen dose to 600 mg, taken 4 times a day. This should help reduce the inflammation and pain.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: Additionally, I\'m going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study to confirm the diagnosis and rule out any other potential causes. If the test is positive, we may consider physical therapy to help reduce the compression or, if necessary, surgical intervention with a carpal tunnel release.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll follow up with the test. What\'s the next step?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll schedule you for the EMG/Nerve conduction study. In the meantime, try the wrist splint and the increased ibuprofen. We\'ll discuss the results and decide on the best course of treatment after the test.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll do that. Thank you for explaining everything.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. We\'ll get you scheduled for the test and follow up with you once we have the results. Take care and see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I\'ll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient: Hello?\n\nDoctor: Hello there. Can you hear me?\n\nPatient: Yes, I can hear you.\n\nDoctor: Hi. Can you confirm your name and date of birth, please?\n\nPatient: Yeah, my name is Alexis Gutierrez. And my date of birth is September 12th, 1990.\n\nDoctor: OK, great. Thank you. Can you confirm your address, please?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I\'m at 123 Main Street.\n\nDoctor: OK, great. Thank you. And can you confirm your medical history, please?\n\nPatient: Um, no medical history.\n\nDoctor: OK. Have you had any allergies to any medications?\n\nPatient: Um, no allergies.\n\nDoctor: OK. And are you currently taking any medications?\n\nPatient: I\'m taking ibuprofen.\n\nDoctor: OK, great. And do you have any regular medications?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any regular medical conditions?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. Do you have any allergies to any foods or drinks?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. Are you currently taking any medications for any medical conditions?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. Have you had any recent illnesses or infections?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. Are you currently experiencing any symptoms?\n\nPatient: Um, yeah. I have some numbness and tingling in my right hand.\n\nDoctor: OK. And where exactly is the numbness and tingling in your hand?\n\nPatient: Um, it\'s in my fingers.\n\nDoctor: OK. Which fingers?\n\nPatient: Um, my first, second, and third fingers.\n\nDoctor: OK. And is that on your right hand?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s on my right hand.\n\nDoctor: OK. And how long has that been going on for?\n\nPatient: Um, about six months ago.\n\nDoctor: OK. And has it been constant?\n\nPatient: No, it\'s been intermittent.\n\nDoctor: OK. And what about any other symptoms? Any pain or numbness or tingling in your arm or neck?\n\nPatient: No, no.\n\nDoctor: OK. Have you noticed any weakness in your hand or fingers?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. Have you noticed any changes in your hand or fingers, such as swelling or redness?\n\nPatient: No.\n\nDoctor: OK. And what about any other symptoms? Any problems with your vision, headaches, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or any other symptoms?\n\nPatient: No.\n\