-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Hernandez is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash. He states that he was unable to ambulate at the time of injury and that a friend had to help him up. He then proceeded to ice and elevate his ankle throughout the night. His pain has since improved and he is able to bear more weight on his right ankle, however he is still limping. He denies experiencing any numbness to his right foot. In addition to ice and elevation he has been taking ibuprofen for his pain, which has provided some relief. He does not recall any other previous injury to his right ankle.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is very active and enjoys playing soccer. He currently plays for the community soccer league.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals signs look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Mild edema\nNEURO: Normal sensation.\nMSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis is present. There is swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. Neurovascular intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\n3 views of the right ankle were taken today. These reveal no evidence of fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture and that he sustained a right ankle sprain. Treatment options were discussed. He was advised to keep his right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. He will also continue to ice his right ankle. An Aircast was provided today to help stabilize his ankle. He is to also utilize crutches for the next 1 to 2 days followed by weight-bearing as tolerated. For pain control he can continue to take NSAIDs as needed. We discussed that he should see significant improvement in his symptoms over the next 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Good, thanks. I\'m here for my appointment.\n\n[doctor] Great. I\'m Dr. Smith. I understand you\'re here for a follow-up on your ankle. How\'s your ankle been since the incident?\n\n[patient] It\'s been a bit better, but still pretty sore. I slipped on the ice while taking out the trash yesterday and fell. My friend had to help me up.\n\n[doctor] I\'m sorry to hear that. How long were you unable to walk after the fall?\n\n[patient] I was unable to walk at all. I had to be helped up.\n\n[doctor] That must have been quite a shock. Did you ice and elevate your ankle?\n\n[patient] Yes, I did. I did that throughout the night.\n\n[doctor] Good, that\'s a good start. How much pain are you experiencing now?\n\n[patient] It\'s improved, but I\'m still limping a bit.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your right foot?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t.\n\n[doctor] Great. Have you been taking any pain medication?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking ibuprofen. It\'s helped a bit.\n\n[doctor] Good. Have you had any other injuries to your right ankle in the past?\n\n[patient] No, this is the first time.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s go through your medical history. You mentioned you\'re quite active and play soccer. Is that right?\n\n[patient] Yes, I play for the community soccer league.\n\n[doctor] That\'s great. Any other medical issues you\'ve had recently?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Okay, let\'s do a quick review of your systems. How\'s your overall health?\n\n[patient] I feel fine.\n\n[doctor] Great. Now, let\'s go through your vital signs. Your blood pressure is within normal limits, your heart rate is regular, and your respiratory rate is normal. Everything looks good.\n\n[patient] Okay, that\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your cardiovascular system. Your heart sounds are normal. Now, let\'s move to your musculoskeletal system. I\'ll check your right ankle. I see some mild swelling, but no deformity. There\'s no numbness or tingling. I\'ll also check for any tenderness. You feel some tenderness on the outside of your ankle, right?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your neurologically. Your sensation is normal. Now, let\'s do some specific tests for your ankle. I\'ll apply some pressure and see how it responds. You feel some pain on the outside of your ankle, correct?\n\n[patient] Yes.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll also check your foot for any bony tenderness. That\'s normal. Your capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, and your dorsalis pedis pulse is strong. Everything looks good neurovascularly.\n\n[patient] That\'s good.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s look at some x-rays of your ankle. The results show no signs of a fracture. Based on your symptoms and the exam, I believe you\'ve sustained a right ankle sprain.\n\n[patient] Okay, what does that mean?\n\n[doctor] It means you\'ve stretched or torn some of the ligaments in your ankle. It\'s a common injury, especially with falls like yours. The good news is that it\'s not a serious injury, and we can treat it conservatively.\n\n[patient] What do I need to do?\n\n[doctor] I\'ll give you an Aircast to wear to help stabilize your ankle. You should keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. Continue to ice your ankle, and you can continue taking ibuprofen for pain as needed.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] I also recommend using crutches for the next 1 to 2 days to avoid putting too much weight on your ankle. After that, you can start weight-bearing as tolerated.\n\n[patient] Okay, got it.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll see you back in 2 weeks to assess your progress and determine when you can return to playing soccer.\n\n[patient] That sounds good.\n\n[doctor] If your symptoms worsen or change before then, please give us a call. We\'ll be happy to see you sooner.\n\n[patient] I will. Thanks for everything.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care and see you in 2 weeks.\n\n[patient] Thanks, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello?\nPatient: Hello?\nDoctor: Hi. Can you just confirm your name and date of birth for me, please?\nPatient: Sure. My name is Gregory Hernandez and my date of birth is November 14, 1977.\nDoctor: Okay. And can you confirm your address for me, please?\nPatient: Yeah. I live at 123 Main Street.\nDoctor: Okay. And can you tell me your occupation, please?\nPatient: I\'m an accountant.\nDoctor: Okay. And can you tell me what brings you to the clinic today?\nPatient: Yeah. I have a right ankle pain and swelling.\nDoctor: OK. So can you tell me a bit more about your ankle pain and swelling? When did that start?\nPatient: Yeah. I had a little slip and fall yesterday on the ice, and I think that\'s when it started. I was taking out the trash and I slipped on the ice and fell down. And I think that\'s when it happened. And I\'ve been having some pain and swelling ever since.\nDoctor: OK. So, how would you describe the pain? Is it a dull ache or is it sharp?\nPatient: It\'s kind of dull and sharp. It\'s kind of hard to describe. It\'s kind of like a dull ache, but it\'s also kind of sharp at times. It\'s hard to describe.\nDoctor: OK. And where exactly is the pain?\nPatient: It\'s mostly on the outside of my ankle, on the lateral side.\nDoctor: OK. And what about the swelling? How bad is the swelling?\nPatient: It\'s pretty bad. It\'s pretty swollen.\nDoctor: OK. And how would you describe the swelling? Is it just on the outside of the ankle or is it all over?\nPatient: It\'s mostly on the outside of the ankle, but it\'s also kind of spreading up to the top of my ankle, just a little bit.\nDoctor: OK. And can you walk for me, please?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: OK. Can you walk across the room for me?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: OK. And how does that feel?\nPatient: It feels like I\'m limping. It feels like I\'m having a little bit of pain when I\'m walking.\nDoctor: OK. And can y