ight ankle. I see some mild swelling, but no deformity. There\'s no numbness or tingling. I\'ll also check for any tenderness. You feel some tenderness on the outside of your ankle, right?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your neurologically. Your sensation is normal. Now, let\'s do some specific tests for your ankle. I\'ll apply some pressure and see how it responds. You feel some pain on the outside of your ankle, correct?\n\n[patient] Yes.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll also check your foot for any bony tenderness. That\'s normal. Your capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, and your dorsalis pedis pulse is strong. Everything looks good neurovascularly.\n\n[patient] That\'s good.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s look at some x-rays of your ankle. The results show no signs of a fracture. Based on your symptoms and the exam, I believe you\'ve sustained a right ankle sprain.\n\n[patient] Okay, what does that mean?\n\n[doctor] It means you\'ve stretched or torn some of the ligaments in your ankle. It\'s a common injury, especially with falls like yours. The good news is that it\'s not a serious injury, and we can treat it conservatively.\n\n[patient] What do I need to do?\n\n[doctor] I\'ll give you an Aircast to wear to help stabilize your ankle. You should keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. Continue to ice your ankle, and you can continue taking ibuprofen for pain as needed.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] I also recommend using crutches for the next 1 to 2 days to avoid putting too much weight on your ankle. After that, you can start weight-bearing as tolerated.\n\n[patient] Okay, got it.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll see you back in 2 weeks to assess your progress and determine when you can return to playing soccer.\n\n[patient] That sounds good.\n\n[doctor] If your symptoms worsen or change before then, please give us a call. We\'ll be happy to see you sooner.\n\n[patient] I will. Thanks for everything.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take 