[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypercholesterolemia.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient denies any history of smoking; however, they note that their parents smoked when they lived with them. They enjoy being outside and riding their bike on trails.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a cousin who died of a heart attack in their 40\'s. but denies any family history of hypercholesterolemia.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure looks good.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No xanthomas noted.\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No murmurs, gallops or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nRecent cholesterol level is reviewed and shows patient is elevated at 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Elevated cholesterol.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s previous tests are consistent with hypercholesterolemia.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends children between the ages of 9 to 11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing well, thanks.\n\n[doctor]: Great. I\'m Dr. Smith. How can I help you today?\n\n[patient]: I had my cholesterol checked and the results were high.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Let\'s go through your medical history to see if there\'s anything that could be contributing to this. Do you smoke?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t smoke.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. Do you have a history of smoking in your family?\n\n[patient]: My parents used to smoke when I lived with them.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Do you enjoy any outdoor activities?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I like being outside and riding my bike on trails.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great. Have you had any family members with heart problems or premature heart attacks?\n\n[patient]: One of my cousins died of a heart attack in their 40\'s.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m sorry to hear that. Does anyone else in your family have high cholesterol?\n\n[patient]: No, not that I know of.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Let\'s go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure looks good.\n\n[patient]: Good to hear.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s do a quick physical exam. Let\'s start with your eyes. Do you notice any yellow bumps on your skin?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t think so.\n\n[doctor]: Good. Your neck is supple and there\'s no swelling. How about your lungs? Do you have any breathing difficulties?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t have any breathing issues.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Your heart sounds normal, no murmurs or irregular beats. Now, let\'s check your abdomen. Any pain or discomfort there?\n\n[patient]: No, my stomach feels fine.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Your liver and spleen are also normal. Now, let\'s review your test results. Your cholesterol level is elevated at 210.\n\n[patient]: Oh, that\'s not good.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it\'s higher than normal. We\'ll do some more tests to see if there are any underlying causes. We\'ll also do a full cholesterol panel, including a hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel. Additionally, we\'ll do genetic testing to see if this is a genetic condition or if there\'s another cause.\n\n[patient]: What does this all mean?\n\n[doctor]: High cholesterol is a risk factor for premature heart attacks. We\'ll work on managing it with dietary changes and other lifestyle modifications. I\'ll also refer you to a nutritionist to help you with a balanced diet that\'s low in saturated fats and sugars.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that makes sense.\n\n[doctor]: The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends children be screened for their cholesterol between 9 and 11 years old and again between 17 and 20 years old. We\'ll also monitor your levels in the future to ensure they\'re under control.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Great. You\'ll need to follow up in 3 to 6 months after meeting with the nutritionist to check your cholesterol levels again.\n\n[patient]: I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Good. We\'ll get started on the tests and you\'ll see the nutritionist soon. Take care and we\'ll see you in a few months.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I\'ll see you then.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and stay healthy. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. Welcome to your appointment today. Can you just confirm your name for me please?\n\nPatient: Um, my name is, uh, Sarah Johnson.\n\nDoctor: Great, thank you Sarah. Um, and your date of birth please?\n\nPatient: Uh, 15th of April 1999.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And your address please?\n\nPatient: Uh, 12 Oak Street.\n\nDoctor: Great, thank you. And your contact number please?\n\nPatient: Uh, 01234 567890.\n\nDoctor: Okay, great. And, uh, just to confirm, are you happy for me to take a few details from you, and then I\'ll just go through your medical history with you?\n\nPatient: Yeah, that\'s fine.\n\nDoctor: Okay. So, are you happy to confirm your date of birth and address?\n\nPatient: Uh, yes.\n\nDoctor: Great. And your name, just to confirm that\'s Sarah Johnson?\n\nPatient: Yeah.\n\nDoctor: Okay, great. And your contact number, just to confirm that\'s 01234 567890?\n\nPatient: Uh, yes.\n\nDoctor: Okay, great. So, can you just tell me a little bit about what brings you to the doctor today, Sarah?\n\nPatient: Um, I\'m just worried about my cholesterol levels, and I was wondering if I could have a check-up to see if it\'s okay.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And, um, have you had any symptoms recently?\n\nPatient: Um, no, I\'ve not had any symptoms, but I\'m just worried about it because my parents had high cholesterol, and I just wanted to get it checked out.\n\nDoctor: Okay. So, y