-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing okay, thanks.\n\n[doctor]: Great. I see you\'ve been referred to me by your primary care provider. Can you tell me a bit about why you\'re here today?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I\'ve been having some pain in both of my knees.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, I see. How long has this pain been going on for?\n\n[patient]: It started about six months ago.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, six months. Do you remember any specific incident or injury that might have caused the pain?\n\n[patient]: No, there was no specific incident. It just started.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. How would you describe the pain?\n\n[patient]: It\'s a deep, achy pain.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds uncomfortable. Where exactly do you feel the pain in your knees?\n\n[patient]: It\'s in the back of my patellae.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Do you notice any swelling or redness in your knees?\n\n[patient]: No, there\'s no swelling or redness.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Does the pain get worse at any particular times or activities?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it gets worse when I transition from sitting to standing and when I go up or down stairs.\n\n[doctor]: I understand. Does the pain go away if you rest?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does get better when I rest.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you been doing anything to try to manage the pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ACE bandage. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve sometimes.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. You mentioned you enjoy running. How far do you usually run?\n\n[patient]: I usually run about 5 to 6 miles a day.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s quite a distance! Have you noticed any changes in your running lately?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I can only run about 0.5 miles now because of the pain.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Have you had any injuries to your knees before?\n\n[patient]: No, no previous injuries.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Do you work from home?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s convenient. Let\'s go over your medical history. Have you had any other health issues?\n\n[patient]: I had an upper extremity fracture when I was in 6th grade.\n\n[doctor]: I see. That\'s a while ago. How about your vital signs? Any changes there?\n\n[patient]: No, they\'re all normal.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. Can you walk for me?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Your gait looks normal, no deformity present. Let\'s check your heart. Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs. Any swelling or edema?\n\n[patient]: No.\n\n[doctor]: Good. Let\'s check your reflexes. Your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical. Now, let\'s examine your lower extremities. Your abduction strength is 3/5, but the remainder of your muscle strength is 5/5. How does that feel?\n\n[patient]: It feels normal, I think.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Let\'s examine your knees. No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. No effusion. Can you feel any tenderness when I palpate the retinaculum?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it\'s tender.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll do a patellar grind test. Can you feel this?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it hurts.\n\n[doctor]: And squatting causes pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Let\'s check the Lachman test and anterior and posterior drawer tests. These are all negative. McMurray test is also negative.\n\n[patient]: What does it all mean?\n\n[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it seems you have bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\n[patient]: What is that?\n\n[doctor]: It\'s a condition where the pain is mainly in the front of your knees, specifically around the patellae. It\'s not due to any fractures or osteoarthritis, which we confirmed with X-rays.\n\n[patient]: Okay. What can I do about it?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll start with conservative treatment. I recommend formal physical therapy to improve your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. You should take it easy on your running for now. You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Any questions about the treatment?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll see you soon for a follow-up. Take care and follow the instructions.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I will.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care.\n\n[patient]: You too.\n\n[doctor]: Bye.\n\n[patient]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello, Sophia. Hi. Good morning. Hi. \n\nPatient: Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hello. Hello. Hi. So, um, so you\'ve got a bit of a problem with your knees, is that correct?\n\nPatient: Yeah.\n\nDoctor: Okay. So, can you tell me a bit more about your knee pain?\n\nPatient: Um, yeah. It\'s been about six months now. I\'ve been having a lot of pain in my knees. Um, it\'s been getting worse over time. And I\'ve been trying to kind of, you know, just deal with it on my own. But I\'ve been having a lot of trouble.\n\nDoctor: Okay. So, so six months ago, you started having pain in your knees. And you\'ve been having pain in both knees, is that right?\n\nPatient: Yeah. Yeah, both knees.\n\nDoctor: And, um, is it in the front of your knees or the back of your knees?\n\nPatient: Um, it\'s kind of... I don\'t know how to describe it. It\'s kind of... I can feel it in the back of my knees, but it\'s also kind of... it feels like it\'s in the front of my knees too.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how would you describe the pain? Is it like a sharp pain, a dull pain?\n\nPatient: Um, it\'s kind of... it\'s hard to describe. It\'s kind of a dull pain. It\'s like a deep pain, but it\'s hard to describe.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And, um, is the pain constant or is it coming and going?\n\nPatient: Um, it\'s