-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle Morales is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain.\n\nOnset of his pain began 2 weeks ago, when he was lifting a box of books and felt a pop. The pain is described as aching and sharp. His pain has been increasing since the time of onset and is beginning to radiate down his left leg. The patient denies any pain with sitting. He adds that he has a sedentary job. The patient states that his pain does not affect his job, however he feels that he does not want to be as active as he was previously. He tries to exercise a few times per week, however he is inconsistent. He denies any numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, weakness, and loss of control of his bladder or bowels.\n\nThe patient has been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for pain, which provides some relief. He has not been evaluated for surgery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nHe denies smoking. The patient has a history of playing soccer in the midfield position.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nThe patient states that his father has back pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\nNeurological: Denies bowl or bladder control issues, numbness, tingling, decreased sensations, or weakness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 130/70 mm Hg.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: I do not appreciate any murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort no respiratory distress. Clear to auscultation, bilaterally.\nBACK: No evidence of trauma or deformity\nNEURO: Normal sensation. Patellar and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the lumbar spine: No ecchymosis noted. Midline tenderness at the L4-5 disc space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Positive supine straight leg test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe MRI of the lumbar spine was reviewed today. It revealed disc desiccation and a disc bulge with a paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neural foraminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar back pain at the L4-5 disc space due to a herniated disc with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and MRI findings today, I have discussed with the patient that his MRI revealed a herniated disc with radiculopathy. We discussed treatment options for this and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of a corticosteroid injection. With the patient\'s consent, we will proceed with a corticosteroid injection into the lumbar spine today. The patient was made aware of the risks of bleeding, infection, nerve damage, blood vessel damage, reaction to the medication, including skin changes, swelling, and also, the risk of elevated glucose levels if the patient were diabetic.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great. I understand you\'re here for an evaluation of low back pain. Can you tell me a bit more about it?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my back has been hurting for about two weeks now. It started when I was lifting a box of books and I felt a pop. The pain is pretty sharp and aching.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. How severe is the pain, on a scale of 1 to 10?\n\n[patient]: It\'s probably around a 7 or 8. It\'s been getting worse since it started.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Does the pain radiate down your leg at all?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does. It goes down my left leg.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Does the pain bother you when you sit?\n\n[patient]: No, it doesn\'t. I have a sedentary job, so I\'m not doing much physical activity.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How about your job, does the pain affect your ability to work?\n\n[patient]: No, it doesn\'t. I can still do my job just fine.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. Have you noticed any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation in your legs or feet?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t.\n\n[doctor]: How about weakness or loss of control of your bladder or bowels?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t experienced any of those.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Have you been taking any medication for the pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol, which gives me some relief.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Have you had any other medical evaluations or tests done for this pain?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t. I haven\'t had any surgery either.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Let\'s go over your medical history. Do you smoke?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t smoke.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. Did you play any sports or have any physical activities in the past?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I played soccer in the midfield position.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, that\'s great! Do you have a family history of back problems?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my father has back pain.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, thank you. Let\'s go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 130/70 mm Hg and your respiratory rate is 18. Everything looks normal there. \n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your cardiovascular system. I don\'t hear any murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Your respiratory system looks normal too, with no respiratory distress and clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Moving on to your back, I don\'t see any signs of trauma or deformity. How about your neurologic exam? Do you have any numbness, tingling, decreased sensations, or weakness?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good. Now, let\'s check your musculoskeletal system. I don\'t see any ecchymosis, and there\'s midline tenderness at the L4-5 disc space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. You also have pain with lumbar flexion. I\'ll do a supine straight leg test. This might be uncomfortable, but I\'ll try to lift your leg gently. Can you lift your straight leg up?\n\n[patient]: Okay, yes.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll lift it. Hmm, you do have a positive supine straight leg test. That means there\'s some nerve compression. Let\'s review your MRI results.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: The MRI shows disc desiccation and a disc bulge with a paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neural foraminal stenosis. This explains the pain and radiating symptoms you\'re experiencing.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see.\n\n[doctor]: Based on the MRI findings and your physical exam, I\'m going to recommend we start with conservative treatment. We\'ll give you a corticosteroid injection into the lumbar spine. This will help reduce the inflammation and relieve the pain.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Before we proceed, I want to make sure you\'re aware of the potential risks. There\'s a risk of bleeding, infection, nerve damage, blood vessel damage, reaction to the medication, including skin changes, swelling, and the risk of elevated glucose levels if you\'re diabetic.\n\n[patient]: I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Are you willing to proceed with the corticosteroid injection?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'m willing.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll get everything set up for the injection. We\'ll discuss further treatment options if the corticosteroid injection doesn\'t provide enough relief. Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. We\'ll get started with the injection right away. Do you have any other questions or concerns before we begin?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let\'s get started. We\'ll be back in about 15 minutes with the results.\n\n[patient]: Okay, thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. We\'ll see you soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: See you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello, hello. Hello. Uh, good morning. Uh, good afternoon. Uh, good morning. Um, can you please confirm your name for me? }} }\n {{ Patient: Uh, yeah. Uh, my name is, uh, Kyle Morales. Uh, Morales. Uh, K-Y-L-E. Uh, M-O-R-A-L-E-S. Uh, Morales. }} }\n {{ Doctor: Morales. And your date of birth? Uh, and your address? Uh, your contact details? Uh, your next of kin? Uh, any allergies? Uh, any medical history? Uh, any relevant medical history? Uh, any relevant social history? Uh, any relevant family history? Uh, any relevant social history? Uh, any relevant family history? Uh, any relevant medical history? Uh, any relevant allergies? Uh, any relevant social history? Uh, any relevant family history? Uh, any relevant medical history? Uh, any relevant allergies? Uh, any relevant social history? Uh, any relevant family history? Uh, any relevant medical history? Uh, any relevant allergies? Uh, any relevant social history? Uh, any relevant family history? Uh, any relevant medical history? U