unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great, I\'m Dr. Smith. I see you\'re here for an evaluation of your left knee. Can you tell me a bit about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I\'ve been having a lot of pain in my left knee. It started about a week ago when I was running around with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop and I think I twisted my knee.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds like it could have been quite painful. Can you describe the pain you\'re feeling?\n\n[patient]: It\'s a throbbing pain. It\'s located on the medial and lateral sides of my knee.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How would you rate the pain on a scale of 1 to 10?\n\n[patient]: It\'s about a 4, but it\'s getting worse.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Have you been doing anything to manage the pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking Tylenol and wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s good that you\'re trying to manage the pain. Have you tried ibuprofen?\n\n[patient]: No, I can\'t take ibuprofen because I have an ulcer.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, I understand. Let\'s take a look at your knee. Can you tell me if you\'ve had any other symptoms besides the pain?\n\n[patient]: No, just the pain.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. I\'m going to examine your knee now. (examines the knee) I see there\'s some swelling and fluid around the knee. It looks like you have moderate range of motion. There\'s pain when you bend and straighten your knee. Let\'s do a few more tests. (performs Lachman\'s and valgus and varus stress tests) Everything looks normal on these tests.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good, I guess.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it\'s looking good. Let\'s take a look at an X-ray of your knee. We\'ll take four views to get a better look. (shows the patient the X-rays) Do you see anything unusual on these?\n\n[patient]: No, everything looks normal.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s what I see too. The X-rays show no signs of a fracture or dislocation. Now, based on the exam and the X-rays, it looks like you might have a mild MCL strain. We\'ll treat this conservatively.\n\n[patient]: Okay, what does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: We\'ll put you in a knee immobilizer to provide extra support for the next week. This will help your knee heal faster. I\'ve also prescribed tramadol 50 mg to help with the pain. You can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Also, I recommend icing your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes each time. This will help reduce the swelling. And for the next 3 weeks, it\'s best to avoid running or any high-impact activities.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I can do that.\n\n[doctor]: If your pain doesn\'t improve with the immobilizer, we might need to get an MRI to see if there\'s any other damage. But let\'s start with this conservative treatment first and see how it goes.\n\n[patient]: That sounds good. Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and follow up in a week. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to call the office.\n\n[patient]: Will do, thank you.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, take care and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Good morning. Good morning. Hello. Can you just confirm your name for me, please?\nPatient: Sure. My name is Angela Powell.\nDoctor: OK. And your date of birth, please?\nPatient: My date of birth is 12th of July 1941.\nDoctor: OK. And your address, please?\nPatient: It\'s 12 Park Road.\nDoctor: OK. And your contact number, please?\nPatient: It\'s 01234 567890.\nDoctor: OK. And your email address, please?\nPatient: My email address is angela.powell@outlook.com.\nDoctor: OK. And your GP\'s name and address, please?\nPatient: My GP is Dr. Smith at 1 GP Surgery.\nDoctor: OK. And your GP\'s phone number, please?\nPatient: It\'s 01234 567890.\nDoctor: OK. And your GP\'s email address, please?\nPatient: It\'s doctor.smith@outlook.com.\nDoctor: OK. So, can you just confirm your name is Angela Powell?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your date of birth is 12th of July 1941?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your address is 12 Park Road?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your contact number is 01234 567890?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your email address is angela.powell@outlook.com?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your GP\'s name is Dr. Smith?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your GP\'s address is 1 GP Surgery?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your GP\'s phone number is 01234 567890?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: And your GP\'s email address is doctor.smith@outlook.com?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: OK. And can you tell me a bit more about your medical history, please?\nPatient: Um, I\'m on blood thinners. Um, I\'m on blood thinners. I\'m on warfarin.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any medical proble